-- Finds the same code twice in Shale's C, from the splitter the C recipes
-- share (cookbook/c/items/, a Roc package), as two kinds of pair:
--
--   twin  two functions of one shape: their bodies' lines, each with every
--         name made `I`, every number `N`, every string `S` and every
--         character literal `C`, share most of their runs of `k=` (3) lines.
--         The score is the share, `structure` (Dice's coefficient over the
--         two sets of runs, 100 when every run of each is the other's);
--         `names` is the same over the lines as written, so a twin with
--         every name changed is `names 0%` and a pasted copy near 100%.
--   run   `window=` (6) or more lines alike as written, in a row, in two
--         functions (or twice in one), as cookbook/zig/find_clones.shale
--         finds them: what a shared function would say once.
--
-- A line counts when it does something: a blank line, a comment and a line
-- of brackets alone (`}`, `} else {`, `default:`) are passed by. A string's
-- text counts in a run (`S("a")` is not `S("b")`), and a comment after code
-- does not. A run within a twin pair is the twin's, not listed again.
--
--   path:first-last name ~ path:first-last name: twin: structure 100%, names 0%
--   path:first-last name ~ path:first-last name: run: 6 lines alike
--
-- Twins come first, highest score first, then runs, longest first. A twin
-- needs `min=` (8) counted lines in each function and a structure score of
-- `score=` (80) or more. A run of lines common to more than eight places is
-- boilerplate, passed by, and so is such a run of shapes for twins. The
-- tests (a `TEST(` body, and every function of a file in a `tests` folder or
-- named `test*`) are left out unless `tests=1`: a test's oracle copies the
-- code it judges on purpose. `top=` (40) caps each list; the counts are
-- of all.
--
--   shale cookbook:c/find_clones.shale            over c/
--   shale cookbook:c/find_clones.shale dir=c/host score=70
--   shale cookbook:c/find_clones.shale self_test=1
--
-- A find is a question, not a fix: two functions may be alike on purpose,
-- as the gate's own JSON reader is kept apart from the host's.
--
-- `self_test=1` runs it over cookbook/c/plants/find_clones/ and holds the
-- findings to the plants exactly: a twin with every name and number changed,
-- a run of six lines in two unlike functions, and their near misses (a
-- function as long as the twins but of another shape, four lines alike
-- that a fifth's string breaks, a test's oracle copy unless `tests=1`).
--
-- It is written in Roc, the app cookbook/c/find_clones/find_clones.roc and
-- the modules beside it (Shape, a function's counted lines; Clones, the
-- twins and the runs; SelfTest, the plants' check), over the splitter
-- package cookbook/c/items/, on Shale's Roc platform
-- (roc_platform/README.md); this file is its launcher. It hands every value
-- it is given to roc_platform/run.shale in the checkout this cookbook is in
-- (the `cookbook` mount's parent), which builds the Roc there (cached; the
-- first build after a change to C Shale compiles the platform's host, 25 to
-- 50 s) and runs the program rooted here, so `dir=` is this root's path. A
-- value the Roc reads no `Shale.arg!` of stops the run before anything is
-- built. `reads=inputs` builds and runs nothing, and prints the real path of
-- each file the program is built from (run.shale's header). It is built
-- with `opt=speed`, as map_file is: the masker reads a line a byte at a
-- time, which Roc's dev build does several times slower than the Shale
-- original's regular expressions; a stop in a speed build names the
-- platform's driver, not the Roc's line. Its goldens, the Shale original's
-- output on the plants, are held by
-- cookbook/c/find_clones/check_goldens.shale.
-- budget: programs=10 output_bytes=8000000

const SCRIPT = "cookbook/c/find_clones/find_clones.roc"
const CHECKOUT = dirname(dirname(real("cookbook:README.md")))
const HERE = real(".")

-- `self_test` is read here by name, as the cookbook's full tier finds the
-- recipes it runs `self_test=1` of. `arg_names()` is every value given; the
-- rest are read by a name made as the run goes, so Shale's own check cannot
-- see them: run.shale holds them to the script's reads.
const SELF_TEST = arg("self_test", "")
local values = {"script=" .. SCRIPT, "in=" .. HERE, "opt=speed"}
if SELF_TEST ~= "" then push(values, "self_test=" .. SELF_TEST) end
for name in arg_names() do
  if name ~= "self_test" then push(values, name .. "=" .. arg(name, "")) end
end
local r = run({"shale", "--root", CHECKOUT, CHECKOUT .. "/roc_platform/run.shale", "--args-stdin"}, {stdin = join(values, "\n") .. "\n", timeout_ms = 1800000, whole = 8000000})
print(trim_end(r.out))
if r.code ~= 0 then fail("find_clones: the Roc program stopped (exit " .. r.code .. ")") end
